course: chinese-street
lesson: 4
title: Yes and no — there is no single word
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: English has one handy «yes» and one «no». Mandarin doesn't: you answer by repeating the verb of the question, so the «yes» changes every time.
explain: Ming asks «shì nǐ de ma?» (is it yours?) and you say shì (it is) or bú shì (it isn't). He asks «yǒu méiyǒu?» (got any?) and you say yǒu or méiyǒu. His granny holds out a bun, «yào bu yào?», and you say yào.
explain: Then there are the playground words: duì (right!), bú duì (wrong!), hǎo (OK!), xíng (that works) and the dreaded parent's bù xíng (no way).
explain: Listen to the question, catch its verb, and give it back: that is the whole trick.
fact: shì («SHR!», falling) means yes, it is, or «to be»; it answers «is it ...?» questions.
fact: bú shì («BOO SHR!») means no, it isn't; bù becomes rising bú before a falling tone.
fact: duì («DWAY!», falling) means right, correct; kids say it a lot, often three times: duì duì duì.
fact: bú duì («BOO DWAY!») means wrong, that's not right; said sharply to a grown-up it sounds like contradicting them.
fact: hǎo («how», dipping) means OK or good, agreeing to a plan; it is not the English question word how, which is zěnme.
fact: hǎo de («HOW duh») means okay, will do, a little more polite, the right answer when Ming's mum says xǐ shǒu (wash your hands).
fact: xíng («shing?», rising) means fine, that works, it's allowed; bù xíng means no way, not allowed, that won't work; whining wèishénme? (why?) after a parent's bù xíng goes down as badly as whining «why?» in English.
fact: yào («YOW!», falling) means want, yes please, and also «will» or «must»; to an adult add xièxie.
fact: bú yào («BOO YOW!») means don't want, or don't! (stop it!); to a granny offering food, bú yòng le, xièxie (no need, thanks) is kinder.
fact: yǒu («yo», dipping) means have, there is, yes we have; it is not the English «you», which is nǐ.
fact: méiyǒu («MAY-yo») means haven't got, there isn't, or didn't; the «not» for yǒu is always méi, never bù.
exercise: Ming asks «shì nǐ de ma?» (is it yours?). It is. What do you say? | shì | méiyǒu | duì bu duì | hǎo de
exercise: How do you say «no, it isn't»? | bú shì | méi shì yǒu | bù yǒu | bú yào shì
exercise: Why is it bú shì and not bù shì? | bù rises before a falling tone | bú is the polite form for elders | It is a different word for no | Only girls say bù shì
exercise: What does duì duì duì mean? | Right, right, right! | No, no, no, never, no way! | Wait, wait, wait for me! | More, more, more, please!
exercise: Ming says 3 + 4 is 8. What do you say? | bú duì | bú yào | méiyǒu | hǎo de
exercise: «chūqù wánr ba?» (go out and play?) You agree. What do you say? | hǎo! | shì de! | méiyǒu! | duì bu qǐ!
exercise: Is hǎo the English question word «how»? | No, it means OK or good; «how?» is zěnme | Yes, it is borrowed straight from the English | Yes, but only in questions | No, it means «who»
exercise: Ming's mum says «xǐ shǒu» (wash your hands). Best answer? | hǎo de | bù xíng | bú duì | méiyǒu
exercise: What does xíng mean? | Fine, that works, it's allowed | Wrong, that's not right at all | I don't want it | There isn't any left at all, sorry
exercise: Ming's dad says «bù xíng» about more sweets. What does he mean? | No way, not allowed | Not now, maybe later | They are not tasty at all | He hasn't got any left
exercise: Granny asks «yào bu yào?» with a bun. You'd love it. What do you say? | yào, xièxie | yǒu | shì | duì
exercise: How do you say no to a granny's food kindly? | bú yòng le, xièxie | bú yào! | bù xíng, no way! | bú duì, that's wrong!
exercise: Someone pokes you again and again. What can you shout? | bú yào! | hǎo de! | yǒu! | shì!
exercise: Ming asks «yǒu méiyǒu qiānbǐ?» (got a pencil?). You have one. Answer? | yǒu | shì de | duì le | hǎo de
exercise: What is the «not» for yǒu? | Always méi: méiyǒu | Always bù: bù yǒu | Always bú: bú yǒu | It has no «not»
exercise: Why does a Chinese «yes» change from question to question? | You answer by repeating the question's verb | It depends on the weather and the time of day | Each family has its own special yes | Only boys use different yeses
exercise: What is the difference between hǎo and xíng? | hǎo agrees, xíng says it works or is allowed | They mean exactly the same thing every single time | hǎo means no, xíng means yes | xíng is only for teachers
test: «just say yes, it's shì for everything» Which is right? | No, you answer with the question's verb: yǒu, yào, hǎo... | Yes, shì answers every question | Yes, but only at school | No, yes is always duì
test: «bù yǒu!» says your friend, meaning he hasn't got any. What should it be? | méiyǒu | bú shì yǒu | bù shì de | bú yào le
test: Ming's granny holds out a plate and you snap «bú yào!». What would happen? | It sounds blunt; bú yòng le, xièxie is kinder | She will think you love it and pile on even more | It means thank you very much | Nothing, it is the polite no
test: You whine «wèishénme?» (why?) after a parent's bù xíng. What usually happens? | It goes down as badly as anywhere | The parent changes their mind at once | You get double sweets | It means «sorry» so it's fine
test: «ming said 'yǒu' means you, like english» Which is right? | No, yǒu means have; you is nǐ | Yes, yǒu is just the English you | Yes, but only in songs | No, yǒu means yes, always and everywhere
test: Your teacher says the answer is 12 and you shout «bú duì!». How does it sound? | Like sharply contradicting a grown-up | Like «well done, Miss» | Like «I don't want it» | Like «I haven't got it, I'm so sorry, Miss»
test: A friend asks «zhè ge xíng ma?» (does this work?) about your Lego plan. It does. What fits? | xíng! | yǒu! | yào! | shì!
test: In a cartoon Granny asks «yào bu yào?» and the boy answers «duì!». Why is it odd? | duì means «correct», but she asked if he wants it: yào | duì is a rude word | duì means no thanks | duì is only said at night
test: «shut up and answer, got any crisps or not» Ming asks yǒu méiyǒu. You don't. What do you say? | méiyǒu | bú duì | bú shì | bù xíng
test: Ming's mum: «zuòyè zuò wán le ma?» You've finished. Which fits the trick of repeating the verb? | zuò wán le (done) | yǒu | bú yào | duì bu qǐ
test: What would happen if you answered every question with hǎo? | Sometimes it fits, but often it would sound odd or wrong | It is perfect Chinese | Everyone would think you are angry | People would think you had said goodbye and were leaving now
exam: Identify the principal manner in which affirmative replies are formed in Mandarin. | The verb of the question is repeated in the reply | A single invariable word signifying «yes» is employed | The question is repeated with rising intonation | Affirmation is expressed solely by gesture
exam: Ascertain the correct negation of the verb yǒu. | méiyǒu | bù yǒu | bú yǒu | bú shì yǒu
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the expression bù xíng. | It signifies refusal, prohibition or impracticability | It signifies enthusiastic and wholehearted agreement with a proposal | It is a greeting reserved for elders | It denotes the absence of an object
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reply bú yào addressed to an elder offering food? | It is blunt, and bú yòng le, xièxie is considered more courteous | It is the most deferential refusal available | It constitutes an acceptance of the offer | It expresses gratitude without refusal
